    "title": "How do we match Automatic Vehicle Location (AVL) data to schedules?",
    "updated_at": "2023-06-02T14:03:05Z",
    "description": "<p data-renderer-start-pos=\"127\">AVL data is received from the vehicles and is automatically matched to journeys in the schedules supplied to BODS. Using various fields in the SIRI-VM data including “OperatorRef”, “LineRef”, “DatedVehicleJourneyRef”, “VehicleJourneyRef” the system will scan the schedules to find a match.&nbsp;</p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"419\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; padding: 0px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.714; font-weight: 400; color: rgb(23, 43, 77); font-family: -apple-system, &quot;system-ui&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, &quot;Fira Sans&quot;, &quot;Droid Sans&quot;, &quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;, sans-serif; text-align: start; text-indent: 0px;\">&nbsp;</p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"421\">If a direct match is found the vehicle AVL data is automatically matched to that journey in the schedules. If more than one match is found (for example when a SIRI-VM packet does not contain any matching journey information) the system will then use a best fit algorithm to determine what journey this AVL data should be matched to. This algorithm uses OperatorRef and LineRef but also uses the vehicle’s current location to compare to where it should roughly be at this point in the schedule. From this it determines which journey best matches the current vehicle's AVL data and will use that journey from then on to record arrival and departure times. Although a sophisticated system, this is of course not as accurate as a pure 1-2-1 match and so it’s always suggested to make sure the incoming AVL data contains as much information as possible.</p>",
    "description_text": " AVL data is received from the vehicles and is automatically matched to journeys in the schedules supplied to BODS. Using various fields in the SIRI-VM data including “OperatorRef”, “LineRef”, “DatedVehicleJourneyRef”, “VehicleJourneyRef” the system will scan the schedules to find a match.      If a direct match is found the vehicle AVL data is automatically matched to that journey in the schedules. If more than one match is found (for example when a SIRI-VM packet does not contain any matching journey information) the system will then use a best fit algorithm to determine what journey this AVL data should be matched to. This algorithm uses OperatorRef and LineRef but also uses the vehicle’s current location to compare to where it should roughly be at this point in the schedule. From this it determines which journey best matches the current vehicle's AVL data and will use that journey from then on to record arrival and departure times. Although a sophisticated system, this is of course not as accurate as a pure 1-2-1 match and so it’s always suggested to make sure the incoming AVL data contains as much information as possible. ",
