    "title": "How do we calculate Arrival and Departure times?",
    "updated_at": "2024-03-12T10:57:40Z",
    "description": "<p data-renderer-start-pos=\"1700\" dir=\"ltr\">As a vehicle travels along its journey the system continually monitors its location against the stops in the schedules. If a vehicle is within a 25 meter radius of the stop it’s considered to be at the stop (and an arrival time would be calculated using the GPS timestamp provided in the SIRI VM data). &nbsp;The system will then continue to monitor the GPS pings until it has left the 25 meter radius of the stop. At this point it will then record the departure time (again using the GPS timestamp provided in the SIRI-VM data).&nbsp;</p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"1700\" dir=\"ltr\"><br></p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"2226\">If two GPS pings happen to “straddle” a stop but both are further than 25 meters from the stops location the system will calculate the arrival and departure times using the geometry of the two GPS points against the stop position. It will then look at the timestamps of the GPS points to calculate when the vehicle was at the stop. In this case both the arrival and departure time will be the same.</p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"2226\"><br></p><p data-renderer-start-pos=\"2626\">Finally the system relies on regular GPS updates to calculate arrivals and departures. If there are infrequent pings and the system determines the vehicle has traveled past more than one stop it will not calculate arrival and departures times for those stops. This is to avoid calculating an inaccurate time because of insufficient data.</p>",
    "description_text": " As a vehicle travels along its journey the system continually monitors its location against the stops in the schedules. If a vehicle is within a 25 meter radius of the stop it’s considered to be at the stop (and an arrival time would be calculated using the GPS timestamp provided in the SIRI VM data).  The system will then continue to monitor the GPS pings until it has left the 25 meter radius of the stop. At this point it will then record the departure time (again using the GPS timestamp provided in the SIRI-VM data).      If two GPS pings happen to “straddle” a stop but both are further than 25 meters from the stops location the system will calculate the arrival and departure times using the geometry of the two GPS points against the stop position. It will then look at the timestamps of the GPS points to calculate when the vehicle was at the stop. In this case both the arrival and departure time will be the same.     Finally the system relies on regular GPS updates to calculate arrivals and departures. If there are infrequent pings and the system determines the vehicle has traveled past more than one stop it will not calculate arrival and departures times for those stops. This is to avoid calculating an inaccurate time because of insufficient data. ",
